---
title: Rice pilaf
description: Lesson 5.1. The absorption method, and what makes rice sticky or separate.
servings: 4
time: 40 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, starch]
lesson: "5.1"
technique: absorption method
status: draft
---

> Technique: the absorption method. The rice is cooked in a measured
> amount of water, with the lid on, until all of it has been taken up.
> Nothing is drained, so the amount of water decides the texture.

Put @basmati rice{200%g} in a #sieve{} and rinse it under cold running water, moving it with your hand, until the water runs clear. Drain it well.

> Why rinse: it washes off the loose starch on the surface of the
> grains. Left on, that starch takes up water during cooking and glues
> the grains together.
-- source: "Rules for Cooking Rice", America's Test Kitchen, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/how_tos/5709-rules-for-cooking-rice

Melt @butter{20%g} in a #saucepan{}(with a tight lid) over medium-low heat. Add @onion{1/2}(finely chopped) and cook for ~{5%minutes}, until soft and not coloured.

Add the rice and stir for ~{2%minutes}, until every grain is coated and glossy.

> Why coat the rice in fat: it puts a greasy barrier around each grain,
> which helps the grains stay separate.
-- source: Mari Levine, "How to Make the Best Fried Rice (When You Don't Have Leftover Rice)", America's Test Kitchen, 2023-05-22, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/3390-how-to-make-the-best-fried-rice-when-you-don-t-have-leftover-rice

Add @water{350%ml}, @salt{1/2%tsp} and @bay leaf{1}. Bring to a boil, then turn the heat as low as it goes, cover, and cook for ~{15%minutes}. Do not lift the lid and do not stir.

Take the pan off the heat. Lay a clean tea towel under the lid, cover again, and leave for ~{10%minutes}. Fluff the rice with a fork.

> Why rest under a towel: the towel soaks up the steam that would
> otherwise drip back and make the top layer sticky.
-- source: "Rules for Cooking Rice", America's Test Kitchen (as above)

> Science: rice is mostly starch, packed in granules. Heated in water
> above about 52 °C the granules swell, lose their crystalline order
> and let out amylose: this is starch gelatinization, and it is what
> turns a hard grain soft. Starch comes in two forms. Amylose is a
> linear chain, 20 to 30% of most starch; amylopectin is branched, and
> in the words of one rice grower it "basically wants to be sticky".
> Long grains such as basmati hold more amylose, so they cook up
> separate. Look up: starch gelatinization, amylose, amylopectin.
-- source: Bashir and Aggarwal, "Physicochemical, structural and functional properties of native and irradiated starch: a review", Journal of Food Science and Technology 56(2):513-523, 2019, https://doi.org/10.1007/s13197-018-3530-2 ; Sawyer Hill, "Do You Really Need to Rinse Your Rice?", America's Test Kitchen, 2021-10-26, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/3946-do-you-really-need-to-rinse-your-rice (interview with Matt Slem of Lundberg Family Farms; stated, not tested)

> Safety: uncooked rice can carry spores of Bacillus cereus, which
> survive cooking. Eat rice hot, or chill leftovers as quickly as
> possible, ideally within 1 hour, keep them in the fridge, eat them
> within 24 hours, and reheat only once, until steaming hot all the
> way through.
-- source: Food Standards Scotland, "Food storage", https://www.foodstandards.gov.scot/consumers/food-safety/at-home/rice

> Next time, without the recipe: rinsed long-grain rice, coated in fat,
> then one and a half times its volume of water, a tight lid, the
> lowest heat for 15 minutes, and 10 minutes of rest.
-- source: "Steamed White Rice", America's Test Kitchen, 2018-01-08, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/recipes/10613-steamed-white-rice (2 cups rice to 3 cups water)
